        {
          "id": 3197666941,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "Also, anything from `tvb_get_uint8` doesn't get tainted because there's no byte swapping that goes on. The taint is generated because Coverity determined that any time there's byte swapping that the data is coming from outside sources and could be tainted. (https://www.blackduck.com/blog/detecting-heartbleed-with-static-analysis.html) But we could perhaps do the same thing where `tvb_get_uint16` is concerned, if that causes taint.\n\nThat also means, I believe, that Coverity is more likely to claim taint when something is in a packet as Big Endian as opposed to Little Endian, since we only generate a report on Little Endian.\n\nWhat I don't understand is why Coverity doesn't understand that assigning a value to a uint16_t is *exactly the same* as `& 0xffff` when it comes to restricting the value.",
